Here's a very basic example of how a brute-force algorithm might look in JavaScript. This is for educational purposes only and should not be used for any unauthorized activities.

```javascript
function bruteForcePassword crackedPassword, maxLength) {
  const charset = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789';
  const charsetLength = charset.length;

  for (let length = 1; length <= maxLength; length++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < charsetLength ** length; i++) {
      let password = '';
      for (let j = 0; j < length; j++) {
        password += charset.charAt(i % charsetLength);
        i = Math.floor(i / charsetLength);
      }
      if (password === crackedPassword) {
        console.log(`Password found: ${password}`);
        return password;
      }
    }
  }
  console.log('Password not found');
}

// Example usage:
const passwordToCrack = 'password123'; // This should be the password you're trying to crack
const maxPasswordLength = 6; // Maximum length of the password to try
bruteForcePassword(passwordToCrack, maxPasswordLength);
```

This script will attempt to guess a password by trying every possible combination of lowercase and uppercase letters and numbers, up to a specified maximum length. It will print the guessed password if it finds a match.

Remember, this is a very simple example and real-world brute-forcing would involve much more complexity, including handling different character sets, dealing with special characters, and potentially using more sophisticated algorithms to reduce the number of attempts.

