```cpp
#define INLINED_CODE(x) x = x * 2;

int main() {
    int a, b, c;
    a = 5;
    b = 10;
    c = 15;

    INLINED_CODE(a);
    INLINED_CODE(b);
    INLINED_CODE(c);

    // Now a, b, and c have been doubled
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `INLINED_CODE` macro takes a variable as an argument and doubles its value. You can use this macro with any variable to apply the same code inline. Note that macros are processed by the preprocessor, so they don't have the same type safety as functions. Use them with caution.

