Certainly! Below is a Java method that takes a `List<Car>` as an argument and returns a `Map<String, Integer>` where the key is the color of the most popular car and the value is the count of that color. If there are multiple colors with the same highest count, it will return the first one encountered.

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class CarStatistics {

    public static Map<String, Integer> getMostPopularCarColor(List<Car> cars) {
        // Count the occurrences of each car color
        Map<String, Integer> colorCounts = cars.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Car::getColor, Collectors.counting()));

        // Find the most popular color
        String mostPopularColor = Collections.max(colorCounts.entrySet(), Comparator.comparingInt(Map.Entry::getValue)).getKey();

        // Return a map with the most popular color and its count
        return Collections.singletonMap(mostPopularColor, colorCounts.get(mostPopularColor));
    }
}
```

In this method, we use a stream to group the cars by their color and count the occurrences. We then find the most popular color by using `Collections.max` with a comparator that compares the values (counts) of the map entries. Finally, we return a map with the most popular color and its count. If there are multiple colors with the same highest count, this method will return the first one encountered due to the nature of `Collections.max`.

